    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a deck build in Vancouver and need to double check some load calcs. I'm using western red cedar for the decking and framing. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load requirements for residential decks under current BC building codes? I need to make sure my beam and post spacing is actually up to spec.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In British Columbia (BC), residential deck load requirements are divided into dead load and live load. Dead load refers to the permanent weight of the structure components, such as decking boards and framing materials like western red cedar. Live load accounts for temporary or moving weights, like people and furniture. BC building code specifies a minimum dead load of 0.33 kN/m2 for decks and a live load of 1.17 kN/m2. When checking beam and post spacing, apply these load requirements to ensure compliance and safety. Remember to always double-check these numbers with the latest BC building code reference or consult with a qualified engineer for specific projects to ensure accurate calculations.",
