      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. so if i'm using 2x10s for my joists, how do i actually calculate the maximum span between them so i don't end up with a bouncy or weak deck? i want to make sure the structural integrity is solid so nothing unexpected happens with the weight distribution.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The maximum span between 2x10 joists depends on various factors such as the load they need to support, the joist species, the spacing of the joists, and the type of decking material. Western red cedar is less stiff than, for example, pressure-treated timber; it affects the deflection limits.\n\n\nTo calculate the maximum span, you should first determine the total uniformly distributed load (UDL), which includes your live load and the dead load of the deck itself. BC building code typically requires joists to support a UDL of at least 1.17 kN/m2.\n\n\nFor western red cedar, you can refer to the Engineering Design Guide for Wood Diaphragms and Trusses (E&DCWG) which provides tables for maximum allowable spans under different loading conditions. If you follow recognized design tables, you will directly find the maximum span for your specific joist size (2x10), span (the distance between support beams), and spacing (how close the joists are to each other). Ensure that the chosen span keeps the deflection at an acceptable level according to BC building code requirements; typically, it should not exceed 1/360 of the span at mid-span.\n\n\nYou should also account for",
