    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden structural failures in decking can occur due to a variety of factors. Common causes include:\n\n\n1. Flaws in material: Defects such as knots or checks in the timber can drastically reduce its load-carrying capacity.\n\n2. Poor workmanship: Incorrectly installed hardware or insufficiently supported joists can compromise the integrity of the deck.\n\n3. Inadequate anchoring: Decks improperly attached to the building or failing to account for the forces exerted on the structure can lead to failures.\n\n4. Inadequate design: Not factoring in the correct spans or loads when designing the deck can lead to deficiencies in load distribution.\n\n5. Extreme weather: Heavy loading from snow, ice, or wind loading can exceed the design parameters and lead to structural compromise.\n\n\nTo prevent unexpected failures, always ensure:\n\n- Quality materials without inherent defects are used.\n\n- Work is performed to recognized woodworking and building standards.\n\n- Deck bracing and anchoring are correctly executed following BC building codes.\n\n- Proper span calculations and safe load requirements are used during the design phase.\n\n- Regular inspections by a qualified professional are conducted to detect potential weak points or damage over time. Ensuring that these measures are followed can vastly reduce the risk of sudden structural failures.",
